WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:05.720
So there are many great books out there for
any entrepreneur to read in general, but I

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:10.780
chose 10 specific books for young or teenage
entrepreneurs to read.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:16.570
And I chose 10 different subjects and topics
for you to read and study before you become

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:20.790
an entrepreneur or if you're already on the
journey of becoming an entrepreneur.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:23.950
So, with that being said, the 10 topics will
be very simple.

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:30.650
Clarity, economy, enemy, your inspiration,
strategy, playing hurt, long-term thinking,

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:34.989
discipline, system and being duplicatable,
and innovation.

00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:37.100
So with that being said, let's get right into
it.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.790
#10: The book is by Peter Drucker.

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:45.359
Its title is Innovation and Entrepreneurship.

00:00:45.359 --> 00:00:48.569
And Peter Drucker in general he has a lot
of great books for you to read.

00:00:48.569 --> 00:00:53.850
If you study Peter Drucker, you will learn
a ton about hiring, firing, marketing, strategy,

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:54.850
advertising.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:58.230
I don't agree with everything he believes
in, but he has a lot of great things that

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:03.659
will make you think and get creative about
what is the formula to being innovative or

00:01:03.659 --> 00:01:06.240
creative, all this other stuff that different
people have used in the past.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.159
He covers that in the book, Innovation and
Entrepreneurship.

00:01:10.159 --> 00:01:13.310
#9: Is a book on system, and duplicatable.

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:17.930
Let me tell you why I think it's very important
for a teenage or young entrepreneur to read

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:21.360
a book on this and that book is Made in America.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.640
It's the story of Sam Walton.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:28.970
The reason why I love Sam Walton the most
when I talk to a young or teenage entrepreneur,

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:31.440
the reason why I think it's the best book
for you to read or study, the best person

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.630
to study, here's a guy named Sam Walton.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:37.010
There was nothing special about him.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:42.229
In 1962 it was the year of the super discount
stores.

00:01:42.229 --> 00:01:44.359
That's when K-Mart was founded.

00:01:44.359 --> 00:01:45.359
Wal-Mart was founded.

00:01:45.359 --> 00:01:46.359
Target.

00:01:46.359 --> 00:01:49.619
All of these guys all got started in 1962.

00:01:49.619 --> 00:01:54.149
And Sam Walton used to own one of these Franklin
stores and then he started Wal-Mart.

00:01:54.149 --> 00:02:01.920
In the first five years K-Mart opens up 250
stores and Wal-Mart only opened nine stores.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:02.970
Fast forward to today.

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:04.049
K-Mart's out of business.

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:06.150
Wal-Mart's got 2.2 million employees worldwide.

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:08.060
He has four kids.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:13.060
Each of them are worth a minimum of $40 billion,
today.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:18.319
If he was alive today he would be worth $160,
two and a half times Bill Gates.

00:02:18.319 --> 00:02:22.710
So you want to study Sam Walton because there's
nothing special about the guy.

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:26.760
He's not somebody where you may study someone
and that guy's an absolute genius.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:30.709
Sam Walton was a simple guy who was a hard
worker and he always looked for ways to make

00:02:30.709 --> 00:02:32.060
his business better.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:34.159
So Made in America for me would be number
nine on the list.

00:02:34.159 --> 00:02:38.280
I almost thought about putting it in the top
five but it's number nine on the list, with

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:39.959
systems and duplicatable.

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:41.080
#8: Discipline.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:44.900
For discipline, I chose Lean Startup.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:50.030
Lean Startup teaches you when you're younger
and you're just looking at business, thinking

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:54.960
I can't believe if I could only get some money
for my mom, my dad, some investor, some venture

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.620
capital angel that gives me a quarter million
dollars, I can start my own business.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:01.120
And you haven't yet tasted the idea of making
your money.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:06.010
If you truly are an entrepreneur watching
this, and you're 13, I guarantee you've made

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:07.519
some kind of money.

00:03:07.519 --> 00:03:09.689
You've sold cookies, shirts, something you've
sold.

00:03:09.689 --> 00:03:14.250
If your blood, if you're one of these folks
that's an absolute entrepreneur since you

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.810
were a kid, you probably already sold something.

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:20.580
If you're someone that's going to be an accidental
entrepreneur from watching something like

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:25.549
this, and you become inspired by it, you got
to learn some of those concepts of making

00:03:25.549 --> 00:03:30.650
the money and how to stay lean so that you
can turn this $200 into $400.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:32.500
And money's all about doubling your money.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:36.360
So how can I make this $400 into $800?

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:41.010
$800 into $1600 $1600, $3200, $6400, $12,000,
$25,000, $50,000, $100,000, 200, 400 million

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.080
dollars.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:46.659
That's a Lean Startup, it teaches you how
to stay very disciplined.

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:51.329
#7: Long-term thinking is a think that a lot
of young and teenage entrepreneurs, especially

00:03:51.329 --> 00:03:52.440
today, you need.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.080
I'll tell you why today.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:04:00.709
Many, many years ago, when there was no social
media or YouTube, or Instagram or Snapchat,

00:04:00.709 --> 00:04:05.790
people weren't as much in a hurry or impatient
as they are today.

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:12.610
So for long-term thinking, I chose Snowball
by Warren Buffett because even though Warren

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:20.590
Buffett today is worth 60, 70 billion dollars,
at 52, he was only worth $50 million.

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:23.770
Now you may say, "$50 million is a lot of
money."

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:26.340
Not compared to $60 billion.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:31.500
So he went from 52 years old at $50 million
to now having $60 billion.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:34.020
It requires a lot of long-term thinking.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:39.710
I think one of the best places I went, I went
to Venice, Italy.

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:43.580
One church I went to that was 330 years old.

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:47.680
And when you were reading the story of it,
it took 80 years to build.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.900
And the man who designed this thing to build,
he knew he was 50 years old, what he wrote

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:55.280
up, he's never going to see the final product.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.990
And most likely, his kids are never going
to see the final product.

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:01.400
The only people that were going to see the
final product was his grandkids.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:06.389
When you study Warren Buffett, you will learn
about long-term thinking where it may be helping

00:05:06.389 --> 00:05:10.360
you in some of the decisions you make, about
hurrying, hurrying, hurrying, hurrying, hurrying.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:12.389
You become aggressively patient.

00:05:12.389 --> 00:05:13.740
You're still aggressive.

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:15.520
But you're patient for your business to be
built.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:18.860
It's a very important concept to pick up as
a young, teenage entrepreneur.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:29.060
#6, playing hurt, I chose Obstacle is the
Way which is a great book by Ryan Holiday.

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:33.229
In this book, he explains to you the concept
of stoicism.

00:05:33.229 --> 00:05:37.700
And stoicism is a philosophy that was born
from cynicism.

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:39.670
A lot of times we call people cynics.

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:42.069
The word, "cynic" comes from cynicism.

00:05:42.069 --> 00:05:46.660
Cynicism used to be a philosophy of people
who would sit around and they would say, "Nothing

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:48.020
good's going to happen.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:51.069
If your wife's going to leave you, odds are
she's going to leave you.

00:05:51.069 --> 00:05:53.350
If you get sick, odds are, you're going to
die.

00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:54.350
Everything was cynical.

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:58.610
So they had this philosophy cynicism and one
of the members of cynicism was a guy named

00:05:58.610 --> 00:05:59.610
Seneca.

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:02.880
He said, "I just can't listen to this crap
anymore" and he started stoicism.

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:07.199
And the first major leader in the world that
picked up stoicism and took it to a whole

00:06:07.199 --> 00:06:10.789
different level was Marcus Aurelius who wrote
the book Meditations.

00:06:10.789 --> 00:06:12.580
He took stoicism to a whole different level.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:16.070
And he ran Rome, he was the emperor for seven
years.

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:21.310
He was loved, adored, everybody loved this
guy, Marcus Aurelius.

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:28.509
And Ryan Holiday takes that concept of stoicism
and explains it in today's world of business.

00:06:28.509 --> 00:06:31.660
And he explains to you that challenges are
going to happen but the obstacle is the way.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:36.970
I think a lot of times young, or teenage entrepreneurs,
the moment you face one challenge or one issue

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:38.210
that comes up people get too distracted.

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:41.340
Oh, you know it's not for me, I can't do it.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:44.660
Which in reality, obstacles are never going
to stop.

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:48.289
The moment you identify that, and you know
that's a part of life, you will look at obstacles

00:06:48.289 --> 00:06:49.289
in a complete different way.

00:06:49.289 --> 00:06:54.060
So Ryan Holiday's book The Obstacle is the
Way is number 6.

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:56.449
#5, strategy.

00:06:56.449 --> 00:07:00.950
For a 14-year-old, you'll look at everything
and you'll look at it from a different lens

00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:05.629
than a 38-year-old looks at, or even a 68-year-old
or even a 24-year-old.

00:07:05.629 --> 00:07:07.949
You're looking at it from a complete different
lens.

00:07:07.949 --> 00:07:12.940
Because your generation is changing so quickly,
with everything, Snap changes everything,

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:16.130
Instagram changed everything, Facebook, Twitter,
YouTube, everything's changed.

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:18.090
You have a lot more access to content today.

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:22.060
I didn't grow up watching games on a iPad,
and my kids do.

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:23.580
I didn't grow up with any of this stuff.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:27.419
I went to school and we had to go to a library
to do research on something with newspapers.

00:07:27.419 --> 00:07:28.419
Today you Google.

00:07:28.419 --> 00:07:30.699
I didn't go to high school with Google being
around ever for me.

00:07:30.699 --> 00:07:32.960
We didn't experience Google myself.

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:38.050
We had AOL chat that came out late in my senior
year of high school So when I went to the

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:39.990
Army, I still listened to Walkman.

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:43.940
When I got out of the Army, it was the first
time I listened to CDs.

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:46.280
And then most of you don't even know what
a CD looks like.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:48.220
Or a Walkman looks like, right?

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:49.220
That's what we experienced.

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:57.069
So for strategy, Blue Ocean Strategy is going
to help your mind think about ways of doing

00:07:57.069 --> 00:08:03.310
business better that maybe a millennial or
a gen x may not even think about.

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:05.639
You're going to see things from a complete
different lens.

00:08:05.639 --> 00:08:09.700
So if you can get the strategy down on how
it's done, you'll have an edge on everybody

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:10.700
else.

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:13.509
So Blue Ocean Strategy, a phenomenal book
for you to read.

00:08:13.509 --> 00:08:15.300
I put that as number five.

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:19.639
#4: Inspiration is Elon Musk, the book is
called Elon Musk.

00:08:19.639 --> 00:08:24.680
It's written by an author who chased him down
and said, you're either going to let me come

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:28.270
and interview you, or if you don't, I'm going
to write you based on what other people say

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:32.950
about you vs. I can come and interview you
and I can tell you what other people told

00:08:32.950 --> 00:08:36.590
me about you and then you can verify it and
give me your story of the version and I'll

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:37.980
tell both stories.

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:39.200
And Elon agreed.

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:42.390
So the author went and interviewed and wrote
the book.

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.270
It's a phenomenal book, good, bad ugly.

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:48.710
And you see how this kid was grown, how he
was raised and how he viewed things and what

00:08:48.710 --> 00:08:52.400
books he read growing up and why he was so
turned on my certain things and who was his

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:56.340
uncle, his father, his parents and what kind
of an impact that made in the way he was raised.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:09:00.100
It's just a very good book to read as a source
of inspiration.

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:02.250
#3, enemy.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:05.380
You need to know your enemy very, very early
on.

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:09.110
And the book I'm going to recommend on enemy
is called Outwitting the Devil.

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:11.950
Outwitting the Devil was a book written by
Napoleon Hill.

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:17.700
I want to say 1929, 1930 that he didn't publish
at that time, and years later the book was

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:19.850
published by a lady named Sharon Lechter.

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:23.850
I think it's Sharon Lechter that the book
was published by.

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:26.150
And the first time I read this book, I'm going
to tell you a funny story.

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:32.150
I read this book literally at the bar of PF
Changs, on my iPad.

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:35.280
The book had just come out, a couple of weeks
prior to that.

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:39.380
I sat there, and I read the book in one sitting
because I couldn't get up.

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:40.750
I couldn't get up.

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:43.520
I called and I said, "I've got to finish this
book."

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:44.580
And I finished the book.

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:48.250
One by one by one I was going through it and
said, everybody needs to read this book, especially

00:09:48.250 --> 00:09:49.860
the younger generation.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:51.910
So Outwitting the Devil will be your number
one enemy.

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:52.910
You've got to read that.

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:54.330
That will be the number three book.

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:58.900
#2: Economy and I was kind of conflicted on
what book to recommend on economy.

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:03.810
I think the best book that I can recommend
to you on economy is not necessarily like

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:08.440
here, go read capitalism, socialism, communism
and realize what it is to hire this person,

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:11.320
fire this person, why you got to let go of
this person, why you want to associate with

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:12.320
this.

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:13.760
Why do some people become wealthy and some
don't.

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:17.200
Who do some countries produce more successful
people than others.

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:19.800
Why do certain philosophies work and don't
work.

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:26.580
So the book I recommend here for you to read
for economy is by Ayn Rand.

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:28.940
It's called Atlas Shrugged.

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:29.940
Atlas Shrugged.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:30.940
Now there's two ways you can do it.

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:32.230
You can actually read the book.

00:10:32.230 --> 00:10:33.580
It's a big book.

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:36.170
It will change the way you view everything
in life.

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:38.020
It's a big book if you read it.

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:39.910
And if you don't want to read it, you can
listen to it.

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:41.840
There's an audio version of it as well.

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:47.090
But regardless, it will change the way you
view every single thing.

00:10:47.090 --> 00:10:50.070
You're going to think about John Galt in a
different way, you're going to look at the

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:52.220
economy in a different way.

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:56.330
You're going to look at the people you work
with, the friends, family, parents, teachers

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:59.960
- you're going to look at everything in a
completely different lens when you read this

00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:01.770
book, Atlas Shrugged.

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:03.990
She wrote two books, Fountainhead and Atlas
Shrugged.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:06.890
I recommend Atlas Shrugged over Fountainhead.

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:09.400
And #1 is clarity.

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:15.490
And I was debating on which book to read,
put this for clarity, and the reason why I

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:20.790
think clarity I put #1 is because when you're
in school, and you don't really know what

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:25.790
you want out of life, and everybody is telling
you how you need to live.

00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:28.100
You need to go to college, get a degree because
if you get this.

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:29.180
. . you'll be successful.

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:36.320
You need to go out there and do this, and
you're still gullible enough or naive enough,

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:38.740
where somebody else can persuade you otherwise.

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:44.440
So what I'm going to recommend you is a complete
180 of what everybody else is going to recommend,

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:45.680
and I'm doing that intentionally.

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:49.530
I'm telling you this in advance, in case you
choose to share this video with your parents,

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:51.560
which is even a better idea.

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:54.690
I'm intentionally wanting to give you the
complete opposite argument, because I know

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:55.690
you're not hearing it.

00:11:55.690 --> 00:11:58.020
For you to get clarity.

00:11:58.020 --> 00:12:03.010
See, if you're raised in a family where everybody
is teaching you capitalism all the time, I

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:06.990
want you to study socialism and communism
to know the opposite.

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:10.670
If you're in a family that's raised communism
and socialism all the time, and everybody's

00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:15.340
a Democrat, I need you to study the complete
opposite side because I want complete opposite

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:17.700
arguments for me to see both sides.

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:19.190
I like that.

00:12:19.190 --> 00:12:20.280
I want to see the complete opposite.

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:25.340
If I'm born in a family where parents say
rich people are bad people, I want to know

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:29.570
why they're saying it, so I want to know why
rich people say poor people are lazy and why

00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:31.590
are poor people saying rich people are greedy.

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:33.380
I want both arguments.

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:35.650
If I only take one argument, I'm naive.

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:40.460
So I'm giving you the second argument of school,
and the number one book I'm recommending to

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:42.970
you is a book called Drop Out and Get Schooled.

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:45.580
I literally just published it last week.

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:49.030
On January 25th the book got published.

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:52.160
And we've gotten thousands of emails about
this book.

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:56.450
It's created a lot of heat and controversy
around the world, not just in America.

00:12:56.450 --> 00:12:57.630
It's a simple book to read.

00:12:57.630 --> 00:12:58.630
It's not a long book.

00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:02.540
There's a 130-some pages.

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:05.350
And it answers the following questions for
you.

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:08.480
#1 is why do we go to college in the first
place?

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:10.130
No one even asks that question.

00:13:10.130 --> 00:13:11.680
Why do you go to college?

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:14.730
#2: Who should go to college and who shouldn't?

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:18.440
#3: Can I succeed and do great things without
